# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_as_map(vx_handle task, vx_handle vmo, uint64_t offset,
                    uint64_t size, uint32_t flags, uint64_t *addr);
vx_status vx_as_unmap(vx_handle task, uint64_t addr, uint64_t size);
vx_status vx_as_query(vx_handle task, uint64_t address, vx_map_info *info);
vx_status vx_as_protect(vx_handle task, uint64_t addr, uint64_t size,
                        uint32_t flags);
vx_status vx_as_key_alloc(vx_handle task, uint32_t *key);
vx_status vx_as_key_free(vx_handle task, uint32_t key);
vx_status vx_keys_set(uint32_t key, uint32_t rights);
uint32_t vx_keys_get(uint32_t key);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A task's address space is the lower half of the virtual addresses, below `0x0000'8000'0000'0000`, and it holds what has been mapped there: ranges of VMOs, the kernel's memory objects (see the kernel's design, 01 §5).
Each call names the task by a handle, so a task can map into a task it is building as well as into itself.
Addresses, offsets and sizes are in bytes and are multiples of the page size, 4096.

`vx_as_map` maps <size> bytes of <vmo>, starting <offset> bytes into it, into <task>.
With `*`<addr> zero the kernel picks the address and writes it to `*`<addr>; otherwise the mapping goes at `*`<addr>, which must be page-aligned.
<flags> is `VX_MAP_WRITE`, `VX_MAP_EXEC` or neither: a mapping is always readable, and is never both writable and executable, so a JIT writes code and then makes it executable with `vx_as_protect`, or maps one VMO twice, once each way.
<flags> may also carry a protection key, `VX_MAP_KEY(`<k>`)`, one the task has allocated (below); without one a mapping has key 0.
<task> needs `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`; <vmo> needs `VX_RIGHT_MAP` and `VX_RIGHT_READ`, and `VX_RIGHT_WRITE` or `VX_RIGHT_EXEC` to match <flags>.
The mapping holds a reference to the VMO, so the VMO's handle may be closed once it is mapped.
A VMO of physical memory, a device's registers, is mapped as device memory; a pager's VMO is mapped as far as its pager has supplied the pages, the rest as they are touched, and a page of it is writable only once it is dirty.
The kernel places mappings upward from `0x0000'1000'0000'0000`, leaving an unmapped guard page after each.
A task holds at most 102 mappings.

On x86, <vmo> may instead be an IoRange with `VX_RIGHT_MAP`, and <offset>, <size> and <flags> zero: then `vx_as_map` lets <task> use that range of I/O ports, its threads from their next switch onto a CPU, and does not touch `*`<addr>.
A task holds at most 32 ranges.

`vx_as_unmap` unmaps the pages of [<addr>, <addr> + <size>) from <task>: whole mappings, or the parts of them in the range.
A mapping cut at one end shrinks; one cut in the middle becomes two.
Pages nothing maps are left alone, so unmapping a range twice succeeds both times.
When it returns, no CPU reaches those pages through those addresses, the kernel's own copies to and from user memory included.
A VMO is freed when its last mapping and its last handle are gone.
<task> needs `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`.

`vx_as_query` fills `*`<info> with the first of <task>'s mappings that ends after <address>: its `base` and `size`, the `offset` into its VMO, and its `flags`, `VX_MAP_WRITE` and `VX_MAP_EXEC` and its key (`VX_MAP_KEY_MASK`).
Asking again from each mapping's end lists the address space, as a debugger or `/proc/N/maps` does.
<task> needs `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`.

`as_reserve` is reserved.
It is meant to reserve a range of address space with nothing committed, at a size and alignment the caller names and a random base, for `as_map` to fill (01 §5).
It has no vx-rt wrapper, and `vx_syscall(VX_SYS_as_reserve, ...)` returns `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` today.

`vx_as_protect` changes the rights and the key of the pages of [<addr>, <addr> + <size>) to <flags>, as `vx_as_map` takes them (ADR-0035).
Every page of the range must be mapped, and <flags> may give each mapping no more than its VMO's handle gave when it was mapped: `VX_MAP_WRITE` only if that handle had `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, `VX_MAP_EXEC` only with `VX_RIGHT_EXEC`; never both at once.
A mapping the range cuts becomes two or three, as `vx_as_unmap` cuts them, so that needs free mapping slots.
When it returns, no CPU reaches the pages with their old rights.
<task> needs `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`.

@node=keys title="Protection keys"

A protection key lets a thread deny itself access to some of its task's memory without a syscall: each mapping carries a key, and each thread holds its own rights to each key, read and write or read only or none, changed by an instruction user code may run (x86's PKU: the `PKRU` register, `WRPKRU`).
It is a guard against a task's own mistakes, not a wall: any code in the task can change its rights back, so code that is not trusted still belongs in another task (sharing(7)).

`vx_as_key_alloc` gives <task> a free key, 1 to `vx_cpu_info.keys` (thread(2)), 15 on x86 with PKU; key 0 is every mapping's default and is never allocated.
`vx_as_key_free` frees one, but not while any mapping still has it, so a key's next owner inherits no protected memory.
Both need `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE` on <task>, and answer `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` where the CPU has no keys: aarch64 today (M6 step 6c5 looks for Arm's permission overlays).

`vx_keys_set` sets the calling thread's rights to <key>: `VX_KEY_READ`, `VX_KEY_READ | VX_KEY_WRITE`, or 0 for none (x86 has no write-only key, so `VX_KEY_WRITE` alone is read and write); `vx_keys_get` says what they are.
They run the instruction, with no syscall, and change no other thread's rights.
A new task's first thread has key 0 open and every other key closed; a thread created by a thread of its own task starts with its creator's rights; `fork` keeps every mapping's key and the forking thread's rights.

A thread's rights hold for the kernel too: a syscall given a buffer the caller may not write (or read) fails with `VX_ERR_ACCESS`, where it would have written (or read) it.
A load or store its rights deny raises `VX_EXCEPTION_PROTECTION_KEY` (exception(2)), with the mapping's key in `vx_exception.key`; under POSIX it is `SIGSEGV` with `SEGV_PKUERR` and `si_pkey`.
A thread diverted to its in-task handler runs it with key 0 opened, and finds the rights it had in `vx_exception.rights`, which the handler's way out writes back (vx-rt's does).
A debugger sees them as `rights` in procfs(4)'s `regs.ndb`, and sets them in its `xregs`.

# EXAMPLES

A stack for a new thread, from the kernel's tests (`tests/kernel/ktest.c`):

```c
vx_handle vmo;
uint64_t base = 0;
if (vx_vmo_create(16ull * 1024, 0, &vmo) != VX_OK) return 0;
if (vx_as_map(self, vmo, 0, 16ull * 1024, VX_MAP_WRITE, &base) != VX_OK)
  return 0;
vx_handle_close(vmo); // the mapping keeps it
return base + 16ull * 1024;
```

